  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on data from a single metropolitan region in Australia, which may limit how well these specific activity-substitution dynamics generalize to cities with different transit systems or cultural time-use patterns.",
      "what": "The study calibrated an extended multiple discrete continuous (eMDC) model using revealed-preference data from a representative sample of adults in Greater Melbourne and Geelong, Australia, to analyze how travel-based activities interact with out-of-trip activities. The findings show that mandatory and maintenance activities performed during travel complement their out-of-trip counterparts, whereas discretionary activities during travel substitute for out-of-trip leisure. The phenomenon of 'activity transfer'—using travel time to free up time later—was found to weaken these complementary relationships and intensify substitution effects.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that travel time is not merely wasted transit but functions as a critical space-time buffer that absorbs excess demand for high-priority tasks and accommodates leisure for time-scarce individuals, shifting how researchers must model the utility of travel time."
    }
  },
